qbone (1.13.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * The web interface announces a newer package when one is published, shows the
    candidate's own changelog since the version the board runs, and installs it.
    The install runs in its own systemd unit, so replacing the service cannot
    kill the install performing it; the new version must come up, answer, and
    still be running ten seconds later, or the previous package is put back. The
    page rides the restart out and reloads onto the matching bundle, and any page
    whose version differs from the server's reloads, so an "apt upgrade" over ssh
    carries open tabs across too. A System page holds the version, the check, the
    changelog and the board's other package updates; "qunilator-update" is the
    same mechanism from the command line. The daily check installs nothing on its
    own, and unattended upgrades are kept away from the emulator package, which
    would otherwise stop a running machine with no warning.
  * A VAX-11/780 runs on the board: the processor is a device of the application,
    its UNIBUS I/O page is on the bus, a device's interrupt reaches it at the bus
    request level it was raised with, and a device's DMA goes through the UNIBUS
    map into the processor's memory. VMS boots from the emulated UDA50 over the
    bus, and the DEC diagnostics run. The dashboard carries the processor's card
    and its console.
  * The board can supply the machine's memory out of its own DDR, one address
    range answered by the PRU. The range is probed before it is claimed, so it
    cannot put two slaves on one cycle, and the memory card draws the address
    space with what answers where.
  * Disk images take copy-on-write overlays: every write since the overlay was
    made is captured beside the image and the base file is never touched. An
    overlay can be discarded, folded into the base, or written out flattened.
  * A PDP-11/34 with KT11-D memory management runs beside the PDP-11/20, chosen
    at startup, and the CPU card shows the status word, the MMU and the bus
    registers. The bus mode is chosen at startup rather than at build time, so
    one binary serves a board driving a backplane and a board that is a machine
    by itself.
  * Disk images live in a folder tree, reachable over SMB, FTP and SFTP, and the
    interface lists the files inside an RT-11 or Files-11 image without mounting
    it.
  * An MSCP disk announces the image's size by default, so a drive type smaller
    than its image no longer truncates it into hard errors.
  * The RX211 carries an MXV-22 mode with 22-bit DMA, which is what 2.11BSD needs
    to read and write an RX02.
  * The DZV11 presents TRDY without waiting on a bus access and clears its write
    latches on Master Clear, so 2.11BSD's transmit loop completes; the DELQA's
    interrupt is modelled level-sensitively, so a completion is not stranded.
  * The DEUNA moves data over the bus, derives a station address of its own, and
    lets a stopped controller sleep.
  * The workstation MCP server runs XXDP diagnostics over the console reliably,
    and reads a pack's directory without booting XXDP.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 16:10:00 +0200

qbone (1.12.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * The web interface and the service's startup banners show the software's own
    product name, QUniLator, the same on the QBUS and UNIBUS capes.
  * The appliance's provisioning tools and their units are named qunilator-*
    (qunilator-setup, qunilator-network, qunilator-leds, qunilator-resize,
    qunilator-announce, qunilator-rename), and its state, configuration and
    asset trees live under /var/lib/qunilator, /etc/qunilator and
    /usr/share/qunilator. An upgrade carries a board's existing state and its
    edited network.conf across, and disables the former bone-* units.
  * The QUNIBONE_* environment variables are renamed QUNILATOR_*; QUNILATOR_DIR
    names the state directory the service reads.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Tue, 28 Jul 2026 07:54:10 +0200

qbone (1.11.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * A TQK50/TK50 cartridge-tape subsystem runs on a shared MSCP port and server
    base alongside the disk controller. It is backed by SIMH .tap images with a
    record layer for read and write, reports end-of-tape, and passes the CZTKBD0
    data-reliability diagnostic with zero errors. The dashboard shows the drive
    with READY, USE and WRITE PROT front-panel lamps that follow tape motion and
    the cartridge's write-lock state.
  * The dashboard grid is arrangeable: every card occupies whole grid cells, the
    operator drags cards and hides them, and the arrangement is stored per
    configuration. The layout editor holds each card's position steady while
    editing, so hiding a card leaves the others in place, and a card dragged past
    the right or bottom edge extends the canvas. Card titles clamp to one line.
  * Emulated mux and SLU serial lines carry over a WebSocket backend.
  * The workstation MCP server reports observable run state, verifies power-up,
    adjusts the log level, and runs XXDP diagnostics. It drives the real console
    one character at a time and confirms each echo, so a multi-character dialog
    value reaches the guest intact.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Tue, 28 Jul 2026 07:00:00 +0200

qbone (1.10.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * The web interface is rebuilt as a Vite + Preact + TypeScript application,
    bundled on the development machine and served as static files by the board.
    It adds URL routing, a PDP-11/03 front-panel dashboard, and a
    configuration-management screen that edits a stored configuration offline and
    then applies it. The running configuration is a first-class object, kept
    apart from live device status.
  * The console keeps its history on the server and replays it to each client on
    connect; terminals dispose and recreate cleanly, and the console and the web
    share one log stream whose level is adjustable at runtime.
  * Emulated serial ports reach the network over TCP: the DZV11, DHV11 and DL11
    muxes listen on assigned ports, retune their line settings while running, and
    advertise WILL ECHO, each carrying a friendly label.
  * The DZV11 and DHV11 are conformed to their DEC technical manuals
    (EK-DZV11-TM-001, EK-DHV11-TM-002) and pass the VDZAD3 and CVDHA XXDP
    diagnostics; each mux holds its own interrupt arbitration slot.
  * The RL01/RL02 subsystem on the RLV12 is modelled to EK-RL012-TM-PRE and
    EK-RLV12-TD-001 — the bus address register, a drive that waits for ready
    before a transfer, the seek state machine, and a controller that defers a
    seek to a busy drive. XXDP boots reliably from an RL pack, and the RL
    controller and drive diagnostics (CZRLG, CZRLI, CZRLN, CZRLJ, CZRLH) pass.
  * A KW11-P programmable real-time clock is emulated (DEC-11-HPWB-D), giving the
    diagnostic supervisor a programmable time base.
  * Each device carries a friendly name and a verbal status; disks report a power
    flag. Enabling a misconfigured device leaves the emulator running.
  * A workstation MCP server drives the board over its HTTP API.
  * The web console drives the real console line reliably: bytes are paced to the
    line's single-character receive buffer so typed input is not dropped and a
    terminal's identification answerback reaches the guest intact, letting
    RSX-11M+ and similar systems detect the console. Several browsers can mirror
    one console at once; the server names one of them the answerer so the guest
    receives a single reply to an identification query, and a reconnect replays
    the history without re-answering.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 16:30:29 +0200

qbone (1.9.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * Name for the bus only what is built for the bus. The emulator binary, the
    unit that runs it and the package carry the board's name; the tools, their
    units and the files under /etc/qunilator, /var/lib/qunilator and /usr/share/qunilator are
    the same on either board. This moves the layout: a board is reflashed
    rather than upgraded.
  * Find the board without hunting for its address: mDNS, a DNS-SD entry
    carrying an identifier drawn from the board's own MAC, a fixed address on
    the USB gadget interface, the address above the serial login prompt, and an
    announcement on the console once the bridge has one.
  * qunilator-rename gives a board its own name, for several on one network.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Wed, 22 Jul 2026 08:00:00 +0200

qbone (1.8.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * Emulate the DEC DEUNA Ethernet controller on the UNIBUS board, with its
    port-command interface and descriptor rings, attached to the host network
    through a raw socket on a shared interface. The emulation has not yet been
    exercised against a running operating system.
  * Share the host Ethernet attachment between the DELQA and the DEUNA.
  * Bound the DELQA's buffer descriptor list walks, so a list that chains back
    on itself leaves the controller idle instead of walking for ever.

